1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,740
This section deals with renal tumors. The first tumor that we want to cover is an angiomyolipoma.

2
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:09,740
Angio means blood vessel, myo means smooth muscle, and lipo means fat.

3
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:17,220
And so this is a tumor, a hematoma in particular, that's comprised of blood vessel, smooth muscle, and adipose tissue.

4
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:26,060
The most important thing to know for purposes of board examinations is that this tumor is increased in frequency in patients with tuberous sclerosis.

5
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:28,240
And that's the high yield for this entity.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,920
Renal cell carcinoma is a malignant epithelial tumor that arises from the kidney tubules.

7
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:39,740
It classically presents with this triad of hematuria, a palpable mass, and flank pain.

8
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:43,300
This is the triad that we all memorize when we think about renal cell carcinoma.

9
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:46,240
But interestingly, all three symptoms rarely occur together.

10
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:49,340
In fact, in only 10% of patients would you see all three symptoms.

11
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:53,000
Hematuria is overall the most common presenting symptom.

12
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:57,620
In addition to this classic triad, patients can also present with fever or weight loss.

13
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:05,840
Now, one of the very high yield points to note concerning renal cell carcinoma is that it can produce multiple perineoplastic syndromes.

14
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:10,380
For example, it could release erythropoietin, causing a reactive polycythemia.

15
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:13,000
It could release renin, causing hypertension.

16
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:17,960
It can release parathyroid hormone-related peptide, causing hypercalcemia.

17
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,700
Or it can release ACTH, causing Cushing syndrome.

18
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:29,100
So, it's very important to note that renal cell carcinoma can produce many different perineoplastic syndromes.

19
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:31,840
Again, that's particularly high yield to bear in mind.

20
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,980
Rarely, renal cell carcinoma can present with a left-sided varicocele.

21
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:39,260
This is particularly high yield because of its anatomy tie-in.

22
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:44,700
Let's recall that if this is the inferior vena cava and this is the renal vein here on the left side,

23
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:48,760
that the spermatic vein drains into the renal vein on the left side.

24
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:52,740
So, if the kidney is here and renal cell carcinoma occurs,

25
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:55,780
renal cell carcinoma has a propensity to involve the renal vein.

26
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:59,040
So, imagine that if it involves the renal vein and blocks the renal vein,

27
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:04,160
that would block drainage of the spermatic vein, which would then result in a varicocele.

28
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:09,680
So, this doesn't usually occur on the right side because recall from anatomy that on the right side,

29
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:12,840
the spermatic vein directly drains into the inferior vena cava.

30
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:18,060
And so, therefore, even if you had a right-sided renal cell carcinoma invading the renal vein,

31
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:20,700
this would not block the drainage of the spermatic vein.

32
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:26,520
Therefore, you don't usually see right-sided varicoceles with renal cell carcinoma.

33
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,480
When renal cell carcinoma occurs, it usually produces a yellow mass.

34
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:35,640
And, microscopically, the most common histologic variant exhibits clear cytoplasm,

35
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:37,920
and hence it's called the clear cell type.

36
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:43,220
Here's a picture of the yellow mass produced by renal cell carcinoma.

37
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:48,480
Again, this is the normal kidney, and this is the fat or the adipose tissue that surrounds the kidney.

38
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:53,500
Here's the histology, again, showing you that there is clear cytoplasm in these cells,

39
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:58,960
and hence the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma is called clear cell carcinoma.

40
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:04,600
Very important to note, and particularly high yield, that the pathogenesis of renal cell carcinoma

41
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:08,600
involves loss of VHL, which is a tumor suppressor gene.

42
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:13,760
Now, when you lose VHL, that increases insulin-like growth factor, which promotes growth,

43
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:17,880
and it also increases HIF transcription factor,

44
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:23,480
which increases both vascular endothelial growth factor and platelet-derived growth factor.

45
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:27,320
So, that would promote both angiogenesis and growth of the tumor.

46
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,020
Now, there are two classic pathways to develop renal cell carcinoma.

47
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:36,940
The first is the sporadic pathway, and the second is the hereditary pathway.

48
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:43,680
In the sporadic pathway, you classically get a single tumor, usually in the upper pole of the kidney,

49
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:45,940
that arises in an adult smoker.

50
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:50,080
And so, again, smoking is the key risk factor for the sporadic pathway.

51
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:54,540
In the hereditary pathway, you classically get tumors that arise in younger patients,

52
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:57,000
and they're often multiple and bilateral.

53
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:04,620
Now, again, both the sporadic pathway and the hereditary pathway involve loss of the VHL tumor suppressor gene.

54
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:11,080
One hereditary syndrome, which can result in renal cell carcinoma, is von Hippolindau disease.

55
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:16,200
This is an autosomal dominant disorder in which there is inactivation of the VHL gene.

56
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:19,520
Now, this increases the risk not only for renal cell carcinoma,

57
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:25,480
but the patients also get hemangioblastoma of the cerebellum in association with renal cell carcinoma.

58
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:30,760
The staging for renal cell carcinoma is based on tumor size and involvement of the renal vein.

59
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:34,480
Remember that renal cell carcinoma loves to go to the renal vein,

60
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:38,820
and this increases the risk of hematogenous spread to both the lungs and the bone.

61
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:41,880
In addition, renal cell carcinoma can go to lymph node,

62
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:46,080
and when it goes to lymph node, it classically goes to the retroperitoneal lymph nodes.

63
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:49,780
Another tumor that can arise in the kidney is called Wilms tumor.

64
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:53,540
For the purposes of examination as well as clinical practice,

65
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:56,680
this is the most common tumor that would arise in children.

66
00:04:56,860 --> 00:04:57,880
The average age is three.

67
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:00,840
So on board exam, if you have a kidney mass in a child,

68
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:02,780
you're really thinking about Wilms tumor.

69
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:05,320
Now, this tumor is comprised of blastema.

70
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:08,940
Blastema is the primitive tissue from which the kidney forms,

71
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:14,220
and this blastema can also produce primitive glomeruli tubules and some stromal cells.

72
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,360
However, the hallmark cell is blastema.

73
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:18,400
Here's an image highlighting a Wilms tumor.

74
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:23,160
All of these small dark blue cells here, they all represent blastema.

75
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:25,680
Now, you can also see that there's a stromal component,

76
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:28,280
and focally, there's also formation of a tubule.

77
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:31,420
However, again, the key component in this tumor is blastema,

78
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:37,080
and it's the blastema that has the ability to form stroma along with tubules and primitive glomeruli.

79
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:41,560
Patients with Wilms tumor generally present with a large unilateral flank mass,

80
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:43,900
classically with both hematuria and hypertension,

81
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:47,560
and the hypertension is actually due to renin secretion.

82
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:53,080
Wilms tumor is associated with WT1 mutations, especially in syndromic cases.

83
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:58,240
One high-yield example of a syndromic case would be the WAGR syndrome.

84
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:04,260
The W stands for Wilms tumor, the A for aniridia, the G for genital anomalies,

85
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:07,280
and then the R for mental and motor retardation.

86
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:11,480
Another syndrome in which we can get Wilms tumor is Beckwith-Williaman syndrome.

87
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:13,500
Of course, the patients would have Wilms tumor,

88
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:17,100
but it would be associated with neonatal hypoglycemia,

89
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:21,200
muscular hemihypertrophy, where the muscles on one half of the body

90
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:23,600
are bigger than the muscles on the other half,

91
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:26,620
and organomegaly, especially involving the tongue.

92
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:29,340
And that concludes this section of renal neoplasia.

